{
  "id": "balanced-binary-tree",
  "questionId": "balanced-binary-tree",
  "slug": "balanced-binary-tree",
  "title": "Balanced Binary Tree",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Tree dfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree",
    "dfs",
    "recursion"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "grind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "checking balance separately from computing height means you recompute the same subtree heights over and over - can you combine the two into one pass?",
    "write a helper that returns the height of a subtree, but have it also signal when an imbalance was found anywhere below",
    "a clean trick: have the helper return -1 as a special 'this subtree is already unbalanced' signal, instead of its real height",
    "at each node, get the left and right heights from the recursive calls; if either is -1 or they differ by more than 1, propagate -1 upward, otherwise return 1 + max(left, right)"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "only checking the height difference at the root instead of at every node in the tree",
    "recomputing subtree height with a separate top-down call at every node, leading to unnecessary O(n^2) work",
    "using an off-by-one height definition (counting edges instead of nodes, or vice versa) inconsistently between the height helper and the comparison"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Binary trees in these problems are encoded as a flat array named `tree` using complete-binary-tree (heap) indexing:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- any position at or beyond the end of the array, or holding the sentinel value `-101`, means \"no node there\"\n\n**A binary tree is height-balanced if, for every node, the heights of its left and right subtrees differ by at most 1. Given a tree encoded this way, return true if it is height-balanced and false otherwise.**\n\nAn empty tree is considered balanced.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= number of real nodes <= 5000",
      "every real node value is between -100 and 100 inclusive",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "empty tree, represented as [-101]",
      "a single node with no children",
      "a tree where the imbalance appears deep down rather than at the root",
      "a completely skewed tree (every node has only one child)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 9, 20, -101, -101, 15, 7]",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "every node's left and right subtree heights differ by at most 1"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, -101, 3, -101, -101, -101, 4]",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "node 2 has a left subtree of height 2 (through 3, 4) and no right subtree (height 0), a difference of 2"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [-101]",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "an empty tree has no node that violates the balance condition, so it counts as balanced"
      }
    ],
